            :readiness "Only after its manager transport, child monitoring, all own anchors and deadline are live does watchdog send nonce-bound READY to controller. Controller must also independently verify its duplicate anchors, manager properties, kernel controls and fresh inventory before GO. Watchdog validates a fixed release request against its own live anchors and deadline, and sends the sole nonce-bound GO over its owned bootstrap control channel; EOF, malformed or duplicate messages, lost transport, process death or disagreement denies GO."
            :failure "Watchdog survives controller death because it is the parent and owns its manager transport and descriptors. On controller death, deadline, protocol failure or pre-GO failure, never release GO; use only a reviewed identity-bound stop of the exact unit it created after revalidating object/invocation and held cgroup inode, otherwise do not signal or stop by name. Rely on the already configured manager RuntimeMax as an independent bound if identity-safe intervention is impossible; record uncertainty, not success. No broad kill, untrusted unit adoption or operator-service action. Watchdog death itself is not covered by its own monitoring; manager limit remains the backstop and an external operator must treat missing report as unconfirmed, not as cleanup proof."
            :proof "Observe both retained pidfds exited and original held cgroup fd link exactly matches the created UUID cgroup plus deleted suffix, with matching held inode, as in V6. Keep anchors until outcome is recorded. Missing evidence, manager/invocation identity loss, timeout or any intervention is an infrastructure failure or inconclusive outcome, never a successful batch. Generic ENOENT or group absence is not proof."}
